(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physical-diagnosis-1-exam-3-pelvic-exam-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Pelvic Exam (Female Genitalia) — Set 2 (Physical Diagnosis 1)","category":"Physical Diagnosis","exam":"Exam 3","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"What is the introitus?","choices":["The vaginal opening","The area between the introitus and the anus","The domed superior portion of the uterus","The external urethral opening"],"answer":0,"exp":"The introitus is the vaginal opening. — Examination of the Female Genitalia lecture, Slide 10"},{"q":"What is the perineum?","choices":["The shallow fossa medial to the labia minora, just below the vaginal opening","The hair-covered fat pad overlying the pubic symphysis and anterior pelvic bone","The area located between the introitus and the anus, and between the medial surfaces of the thighs","The thin membrane partially covering the introitus in a virginal female before intercourse"],"answer":2,"exp":"The perineum is the area between the vaginal introitus and the anus, bounded medially by the thighs. — Examination of the Female Genitalia lecture, Slide 10"},{"q":"Where is the urethral (external) meatus located relative to the clitoris?","choices":["Directly within the vaginal introitus itself, sharing a single common opening","Anterior to the clitoris, just beneath the clitoral hood and prepuce","Lateral to the labia majora, near the inguinal skin folds","Posterior to the clitoris, as a slightly vertical, elongated orifice"],"answer":3,"exp":"The urethral meatus is a slightly vertical, elongated orifice located posterior to the clitoris. — Examination of the Female Genitalia lecture, Slide 11"},{"q":"What is the clitoral prepuce?","choices":["The periurethral glands opening on either side of the urethral meatus","The rounded folds of adipose and connective tissue extending from the mons pubis","The cutaneous covering of the clitoris, sometimes called the female foreskin","The erectile glans tissue at the tip of the clitoris, richly innervated"],"answer":2,"exp":"The prepuce is the cutaneous covering of the clitoris, analogous to the foreskin. — Examination of the Female Genitalia lecture, Slide 9"},{"q":"How far do the labia majora extend?","choices":["Only from the clitoral hood down to the urethral meatus","From the mons pubis to just past the posterior commissure","Only from the anus forward to the perineal body","Only within the vestibule, medial to the labia minora"],"answer":1,"exp":"The labia majora are rounded folds of adipose and connective tissue extending from the mons pubis to just past the posterior commissure. — Examination of the Female Genitalia lecture, Slide 8"},{"q":"How is the uterine body normally positioned relative to the vaginal wall and bladder?","choices":["Almost at a right angle with the vaginal wall, inclined slightly forward over the bladder","Nearly parallel to the vaginal wall, inclined backward and downward over the rectum","There is no typical positional relationship, since uterine position varies randomly","Directly posterior to the rectum, angled sharply away from the bladder"],"answer":0,"exp":"The uterine body sits almost at a right angle to the vaginal wall and is inclined slightly forward (anteverted) over the bladder. — Examination of the Female Genitalia lecture, Slide 14"},{"q":"What shape does the external cervical os typically have in a nulliparous woman versus after childbirth?","choices":["Always slit-like regardless of childbirth history","Slit-like in the nulliparous woman; round after childbirth","Always round regardless of childbirth history","Round in the nulliparous woman; slit-like after childbirth"],"answer":3,"exp":"The external os is typically round before childbirth and becomes slit-like after vaginal delivery. — Examination of the Female Genitalia lecture, Slide 15"},{"q":"What epithelium types meet at the cervical external os?","choices":["Only squamous epithelium continuous with the vaginal wall, with no columnar component present anywhere","Transitional epithelium exclusively, identical to that lining the urinary bladder and ureters","Only columnar epithelium continuous with the cervical canal, with no squamous component present","Squamous epithelium continuous with the vaginal wall, and columnar epithelium continuous with the cervical canal lining"],"answer":3,"exp":"The squamocolumnar junction at the os marks the transition from vaginal squamous epithelium to endocervical columnar epithelium. — Examination of the Female Genitalia lecture, Slide 15"},{"q":"What are rugae, and when are they typically absent?","choices":["Folds of the vaginal mucosa, absent in children and post-menopausal females","The fan-like fimbriated ends found at the distal fallopian tubes","Pigmented patches of skin scattered across the labia majora","Folds of the vaginal mucosa, absent only during active pregnancy and lactation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Vaginal rugae are mucosal folds that are absent in children and post-menopausal women (states of lower estrogen). — Examination of the Female Genitalia lecture, Slide 13"},{"q":"What is distinctive about the distal ends of the fallopian tubes?","choices":["They contain the squamocolumnar junction","They open directly into the peritoneal cavity via the cervix","They are fan-like structures called fimbriated ends","They are directly fused to the ovarian surface"],"answer":2,"exp":"The distal ends of the fallopian tubes are fan-like, fimbriated structures located at the upper lateral angles of the uterus. — Examination of the Female Genitalia lecture, Slide 16"},{"q":"Why is the patient's head and shoulders elevated during the pelvic exam?","choices":["It improves the examiner's line of sight to the cervix","It helps relax the abdominal muscles","It reduces the risk of vasovagal syncope","It is required for speculum insertion"],"answer":1,"exp":"Elevating the head and shoulders helps relax the abdominal musculature, aiding the exam. — Examination of the Female Genitalia lecture, Slide 19"},{"q":"What is the purpose of offering a mirror during the pelvic exam?","choices":["It is required for accurate cervical inspection","It is used to demonstrate speculum insertion technique","It substitutes for the room's overhead lighting","It allows the woman to view the exam, helping her relax"],"answer":3,"exp":"Offering a mirror lets the patient view the exam, which can help her relax. — Examination of the Female Genitalia lecture, Slide 20"},{"q":"What equipment is needed for a Pap smear as part of the pelvic exam?","choices":["A tuning fork and an otoscope for ear and hearing tests","Only a speculum and a small tube of lubricant gel","Cotton swab, spatula, brush, glass slide, and fixative spray","Only a cotton swab and a single glass microscope slide"],"answer":2,"exp":"Pap smear equipment includes a cotton swab, spatula, brush, glass slide, and fixative spray. — Examination of the Female Genitalia lecture, Slide 21"},{"q":"Why should each step of the pelvic exam be explained to the patient before proceeding?","choices":["It is part of the general approach to reduce patient anxiety and build trust during a sensitive exam","It is a legal requirement mandated by the state medical board, with no additional clinical benefit","It replaces the need for draping, since verbal explanation alone maintains adequate patient privacy","It is only necessary if a chaperone is not present in the room during the examination"],"answer":0,"exp":"Explaining each step before proceeding, along with smooth movements, is part of the general technique to keep the patient informed and comfortable. — Examination of the Female Genitalia lecture, Slide 20"},{"q":"Why is a pen light kept available as pelvic exam equipment?","choices":["It is not part of the recommended equipment list","To use if the speculum does not have its own light adapter","To sterilize the speculum's metal surfaces before each use","To check the patient's pupillary reaction before starting the exam"],"answer":1,"exp":"A pen light is available specifically for cases where the speculum lacks a built-in light adapter. — Examination of the Female Genitalia lecture, Slide 21"},{"q":"On external inspection, what findings should be noted regarding the mons pubis and labia majora?","choices":["Only the color and coarseness of the pubic hair","Only the skin temperature over the mons and labia","Inflammation, ulcerations, discharge, nodules, and vesicles","Only whether a chaperone is present in the room"],"answer":2,"exp":"These are the specific findings to note when inspecting the mons pubis and labia majora. — Examination of the Female Genitalia lecture, Slide 22"},{"q":"How should the labia majora be manipulated during inspection?","choices":["Retracted only after the speculum has already been inserted","Left untouched, relying on visual inspection alone","Pressed firmly together to occlude the introitus","Gently retracted laterally to expose underlying structures"],"answer":3,"exp":"The labia majora are gently retracted laterally to expose the structures beneath during inspection. — Examination of the Female Genitalia lecture, Slide 22"},{"q":"According to Tanner staging of pubarche, what characterizes Stage 1?","choices":["Hair adult in type but covering a smaller area, with no spread to the medial thighs","Sparse growth of long, pigmented hair at the base of the labia","Prepubertal, with vellus hair similar to the abdominal wall","Adult in type and quantity, with horizontal distribution"],"answer":2,"exp":"Stage 1 pubarche is prepubertal, showing only fine vellus hair similar to that on the abdominal wall. — Examination of the Female Genitalia lecture, Slide 23"},{"q":"What structures are identified after separating the labia minora?","choices":["Clitoris, urethral meatus, and introitus","Only the ovaries, deep within the pelvis","Only the cervix, visualized with a speculum","Only the fallopian tubes and their fimbriae"],"answer":0,"exp":"Separating the labia minora exposes the clitoris, urethral meatus, and vaginal introitus for inspection. — Examination of the Female Genitalia lecture, Slide 25"},{"q":"According to Tanner staging of pubarche, what characterizes Stage 5?","choices":["Prepubertal, with only vellus hair","Darker, coarser hair spreading sparsely over the pubic junction","Adult in type and quantity, with horizontal (\"feminine\") distribution","Hair adult in type but covering a smaller area than an adult, no spread to the thighs"],"answer":2,"exp":"Stage 5 pubarche is adult in type and quantity with a horizontal distribution pattern. — Examination of the Female Genitalia lecture, Slide 23"},{"q":"How is the vaginal introitus enlarged prior to speculum insertion?","choices":["Inserting the speculum fully open from the very first moment","No manual preparation is needed before speculum insertion","Applying upward pressure with one finger on the anterior margin","Applying downward pressure with two fingers on the posterior margin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Downward pressure with two fingers on the posterior introital margin enlarges the opening prior to speculum insertion. — Examination of the Female Genitalia lecture, Slide 29"},{"q":"For the endocervical Pap smear specimen, how is the cotton swab used?","choices":["Rotated within the vagina only, then discarded without ever being smeared onto a slide for cytologic review","Inserted into the cervical os, rolled between fingers, and smeared onto the slide with a painting motion, then fixed","Used to collect a squamocolumnar specimen by scraping the ectocervix in a full circular motion","Rubbed externally over the ectocervix surface only, never advanced into the cervical os itself"],"answer":1,"exp":"This is the correct cotton swab technique for obtaining and preparing the endocervical specimen. — Examination of the Female Genitalia lecture, Slide 35"},{"q":"What specimen does the cervical scrape technique obtain, and how is it performed?","choices":["A vaginal wall specimen, obtained by swabbing along both lateral vaginal wall surfaces","No specific technique matters, since any brief contact with the cervical surface suffices","An endocervical specimen, obtained by gently rolling a cotton swab within the cervical os","A squamocolumnar specimen, by inserting the long end into the os, pressing slightly, and scraping in a full circle"],"answer":3,"exp":"The cervical scrape obtains a squamocolumnar junction specimen via a full-circle scraping motion after inserting the long end into the os. — Examination of the Female Genitalia lecture, Slide 35"},{"q":"After cervical inspection and specimen collection, how is the speculum withdrawn and what is inspected during withdrawal?","choices":["Withdraw quickly while fully closed, skipping any additional inspection of the vaginal walls","Withdraw only after fully reinserting and reopening the same speculum a second time","The vaginal walls cannot be inspected during withdrawal, since visibility is too limited","Withdraw slowly in the open position, inspecting the vaginal walls for color, discharge, and ulcerations"],"answer":3,"exp":"The speculum is withdrawn slowly while still open, allowing inspection of the vaginal walls for color, discharge, and ulcerations. — Examination of the Female Genitalia lecture, Slide 38"},{"q":"How is the cervical brush specimen obtained?","choices":["Scrape in a full circle around the external os without insertion","Swab the vaginal fornices only","Press firmly against the ectocervix without entering the os","Insert into the cervical os and rotate the tip"],"answer":3,"exp":"The cervical brush technique involves inserting it into the os and rotating the tip to collect cells. — Examination of the Female Genitalia lecture, Slide 38"},{"q":"During the bimanual exam, what is assessed when palpating the vaginal wall?","choices":["Only the presence of rugae","Nothing; the vaginal wall is not palpated in the bimanual exam","Tenderness, nodules, and similar findings","Only vaginal wall length"],"answer":2,"exp":"The vaginal wall is palpated for tenderness, nodules, and similar abnormal findings during the bimanual exam. — Examination of the Female Genitalia lecture, Slide 39"},{"q":"During the rectovaginal exam, which finger is introduced into the vagina and which into the anus?","choices":["Only the index finger is used, entering both orifices sequentially","Index finger into the vagina, middle finger into the anus","Middle finger into the vagina, index finger into the anus","Both index and middle fingers into the vagina only"],"answer":1,"exp":"The index finger is placed in the vagina while the middle finger is introduced into the anus for the rectovaginal exam. — Examination of the Female Genitalia lecture, Slide 44"},{"q":"During the rectovaginal exam, what should the patient be asked to do as the middle finger enters the anus?","choices":["Hold their breath","Cough forcefully","Fully relax with no active maneuver","Bear down as if defecating"],"answer":3,"exp":"Asking the patient to bear down as if defecating during anal finger insertion eases entry and relaxes the sphincter. — Examination of the Female Genitalia lecture, Slide 44"},{"q":"What structures are specifically palpated during the rectovaginal exam?","choices":["The rectovaginal septum and the posterior fornix","Only the labia majora, assessed externally","Only the urethral meatus and its opening","Only the anterior fornix, near the bladder"],"answer":0,"exp":"The rectovaginal exam specifically palpates the rectovaginal septum and posterior fornix. — Examination of the Female Genitalia lecture, Slide 44"},{"q":"At the end of the rectovaginal exam, what should be checked while withdrawing the rectal finger?","choices":["Prostate size","Nothing further is assessed at this point","Sphincter tone","Bladder distension"],"answer":2,"exp":"Sphincter tone is checked while withdrawing the rectal finger at the conclusion of the rectovaginal exam. — Examination of the Female Genitalia lecture, Slide 44"}]};
